\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The same positive scale factor $\lambda =1+t/t_0$, $t_0\in \mathbb  {R}_{>0}$, used as an exact coordinate rescaling (left) or an assigned local displacement (right). Both diagrams use the same chart, with $r_2=r_3=0$. Thick cyan curves are light rays emitted from the origin; short black arrows show local light directions. Curved coordinate paths occur in both panels and do not establish spacetime curvature. Here $K\in \mathbb  {R}_{\ge 0}$ is the Kretschmann scalar of the full four-dimensional metric, as in \eqref  {eq:matrix-basis-curvature}: only the right metric has nonzero curvature, even though $\ddot  \lambda =0$.}}{89}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:coordinates-curvature}{{7}{89}{The same positive scale factor $\lambda =1+t/t_0$, $t_0\in \R _{>0}$, used as an exact coordinate rescaling (left) or an assigned local displacement (right). Both diagrams use the same chart, with $r_2=r_3=0$. Thick cyan curves are light rays emitted from the origin; short black arrows show local light directions. Curved coordinate paths occur in both panels and do not establish spacetime curvature. Here $K\in \R _{\ge 0}$ is the Kretschmann scalar of the full four-dimensional metric, as in \eqref {eq:matrix-basis-curvature}: only the right metric has nonzero curvature, even though $\ddot \lambda =0$}{figure.7}{}}
